const windowsSecurityUsersGroupsAccountManagementLessonContent = "# Windows Security: Windows Users, Groups and Account Management\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nBy the end of this lesson, you should be able to:\n\n1. Explain Windows user accounts and their importance in security.\n2. Differentiate between local, Microsoft, domain and service accounts.\n3. Understand Windows account types and privileges.\n4. Explain how Windows security groups work.\n5. Identify important built-in Windows groups.\n6. Create, modify, disable and delete local user accounts.\n7. Add and remove users from security groups.\n8. Understand account Security Identifiers (SIDs).\n9. Configure password and account lockout policies.\n10. Inspect user accounts using PowerShell and Command Prompt.\n11. Apply least privilege and account-management security practices.\n12. Investigate suspicious account activity.\n\n---\n\n# PART 1: UNDERSTANDING WINDOWS USERS AND ACCOUNTS\n\n## 1.1 What Is a Windows User Account?\n\nA Windows user account is an identity that allows a person or service to interact with a Windows computer.\n\nEach account has an identity and an associated security context.\n\nWindows uses this information to determine:\n\n- Who is accessing the computer.\n- Which resources the user can access.\n- Which applications the user can execute.\n- Which administrative operations the user can perform.\n- Which security policies apply to the account.\n\nFor example, imagine a company with five employees using Windows computers.\n\nEach employee should have an individual account rather than sharing one common account.\n\n| Employee | Username | Responsibility |\n|---|---|---|\n| Brian | brian | Software development |\n| Mary | mary | Accounting |\n| John | john | Customer support |\n| Peter | peter | Administration |\n| Grace | grace | Human resources |\n\nEach employee can receive permissions appropriate to their responsibilities.\n\n### Why should users have individual accounts?\n\nIndividual accounts provide:\n\n1. **Accountability:** Activities can be associated with specific identities.\n2. **Access control:** Each user receives appropriate permissions.\n3. **Auditing:** Administrators can investigate who performed an action.\n4. **Privacy:** Users can have separate files and settings.\n5. **Security:** Compromise of one account does not automatically require sharing the credentials of every other employee.\n\nSharing administrator credentials makes it difficult to determine who performed particular actions.\n\n## 1.2 Types of Windows User Accounts\n\nWindows supports different account models depending on the environment.\n\n### A. Local User Accounts\n\nA local account is created and managed on an individual Windows computer.\n\nIt is commonly used on:\n\n- Personal computers.\n- Standalone workstations.\n- Laboratory computers.\n- Computers that are not centrally managed.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nComputer: DESKTOP-01\nUsername: brian\n```\n\nThe account exists locally on that computer.\n\nA local account can sign in without requiring a Microsoft online account.\n\n### B. Microsoft Accounts\n\nA Microsoft account connects Windows sign-in with Microsoft's online account services.\n\nDepending on configuration, it can support:\n\n- Synchronization of selected settings.\n- Access to Microsoft services.\n- Cloud-connected features.\n- Account recovery.\n- Integration with supported applications.\n\nA Microsoft account is different from a traditional local Windows account, although Windows can use a Microsoft identity to establish a local sign-in profile.\n\n### C. Domain User Accounts\n\nDomain accounts are commonly used in organizations that centrally manage identities through Active Directory Domain Services (AD DS).\n\nAn administrator can create an account once in the organization's directory and use policies to manage access across multiple computers.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nCompany Domain: firebox.local\n\nUser: FIREBOX\\brian\nUser: FIREBOX\\mary\nUser: FIREBOX\\john\n```\n\nThe actual domain name depends on the organization's configuration.\n\n### Domain account architecture\n\n```text\n             COMPANY NETWORK\n                    |\n                    v\n          ACTIVE DIRECTORY DOMAIN\n                    |\n             DOMAIN CONTROLLER\n                    |\n        +-----------+-----------+\n        |           |           |\n        v           v           v\n      Brian       Mary        John\n        |           |           |\n        v           v           v\n      PC-01       PC-02       PC-03\n```\n\nThe domain controller helps authenticate domain identities and provide centralized directory services.\n\nThis is especially useful in organizations with many users and computers.\n\n### D. Service Accounts\n\nA service account is an identity used by a background service or application.\n\nFor example, a database service may use a dedicated identity to access database files.\n\nService accounts should receive only the permissions needed by the service.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- Application service accounts.\n- Database service identities.\n- Scheduled-task identities.\n- Managed service accounts in supported Active Directory environments.\n\nA service should not automatically run with full administrator privileges.\n\n### E. Built-in Accounts\n\nWindows also provides built-in accounts.\n\nExamples:\n\n| Account | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| Administrator | Built-in administrative account. |\n| Guest | Restricted account intended for guest access; commonly disabled. |\n| SYSTEM | Highly privileged operating system identity. |\n| LOCAL SERVICE | Built-in identity for services requiring limited local privileges. |\n| NETWORK SERVICE | Built-in identity for services that may authenticate to network resources as the computer. |\n\nThese identities have different purposes and security characteristics.\n\nThe SYSTEM, LOCAL SERVICE and NETWORK SERVICE identities are not ordinary interactive employee accounts.\n\n## 1.3 Standard Users and Administrators\n\nWindows commonly distinguishes between standard users and administrators.\n\n### A. Standard User\n\nA standard user is intended for ordinary daily computer activities.\n\nTypical activities include:\n\n- Opening applications.\n- Creating documents.\n- Browsing the internet.\n- Accessing permitted folders.\n- Changing certain personal settings.\n\nStandard users generally cannot make unrestricted system-wide changes.\n\n### B. Administrator\n\nAn administrator can perform privileged system-management operations.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- Installing system-wide software.\n- Managing local user accounts.\n- Changing system security settings.\n- Configuring device drivers.\n- Managing services.\n- Modifying protected system files.\n\nAdministrative permissions should be restricted to people who genuinely need them.\n\n### Standard user vs. administrator\n\n| Feature | Standard User | Administrator |\n|---|---|---|\n| Run ordinary applications | Yes | Yes |\n| Create personal documents | Yes | Yes |\n| Install applications for the current user | Depends on application and configuration | Yes |\n| Change system-wide security settings | Restricted | Generally permitted when elevated |\n| Manage other user accounts | Restricted | Yes, subject to controls |\n| Modify protected system files | Restricted | Can do so when appropriately elevated |\n\n### Understanding UAC and administrator accounts\n\nA user belonging to the Administrators group does not necessarily run every application with full administrative privileges.\n\nOn systems using User Account Control, an administrator commonly receives a filtered token for normal activity.\n\nWhen elevation is needed, Windows can request approval or administrative credentials.\n\nThis reduces the risk of every application automatically running with elevated privileges.\n\n## 1.4 Understanding Windows Security Identifiers (SIDs)\n\nWindows uses Security Identifiers to uniquely identify accounts and groups.\n\nA SID is not simply a username.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nS-1-5-21-123456789-987654321-123456789-1001\n```\n\nThis is an illustrative SID.\n\nA SID identifies a security principal independently of its displayed account name.\n\n### Why are SIDs important?\n\nImagine an employee changes their username from:\n\n```text\nbrian\n```\n\nto:\n\n```text\nbrian.njogu\n```\n\nWindows can continue using the same SID to identify the underlying account.\n\nThis helps preserve security references associated with the identity.\n\n### SID structure\n\n| Component | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| S | Indicates a Security Identifier. |\n| 1 | SID revision. |\n| 5 | Identifier authority. |\n| Remaining values | Subauthority values and account identifier. |\n\nThe final number is commonly the Relative Identifier (RID).\n\n### Practical: Find your SID\n\nOpen PowerShell and run:\n\n```powershell\nwhoami /user\n```\n\nExample output:\n\n```text\nUSER INFORMATION\n\nUser Name          SID\n------------------ --------------------------------------------\nDESKTOP-01\\brian   S-1-5-21-123456789-987654321-123456789-1001\n```\n\nYour actual SID will be different.\n\nYou can also inspect a particular local user:\n\n```powershell\nGet-LocalUser -Name \"brian\" | Select-Object Name, SID\n```\n\nReplace `brian` with an existing local username.\n\n## 1.5 User Profiles and Home Directories\n\nWhen a person signs into Windows, the operating system loads their user profile.\n\nA profile stores user-specific information, such as:\n\n- Desktop settings.\n- Documents.\n- Downloads.\n- Application preferences.\n- User-specific registry settings.\n- Other personal configuration data.\n\nA typical profile path looks like:\n\n```text\nC:\\Users\\brian\\\n```\n\n### Common user profile folders\n\n| Folder | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| Desktop | User desktop files. |\n| Documents | Personal documents. |\n| Downloads | Downloaded files. |\n| Pictures | Image files. |\n| Music | Audio files. |\n| Videos | Video files. |\n| AppData | Application-specific user data and settings. |\n\n### Why are user profiles important in security?\n\nUser profiles may contain sensitive information, including:\n\n- Application configuration.\n- Browser data.\n- Authentication-related data.\n- Cached information.\n- Personal files.\n\nAn attacker who compromises a user account may attempt to access data stored in that user's profile.\n\nTherefore, user profiles should be protected through appropriate account permissions, endpoint security and encryption.\n\n## 1.6 Windows Logon Sessions\n\nA logon session is created when Windows successfully authenticates an identity.\n\nThe session provides a security context for the user's activity.\n\nThis context includes information such as:\n\n- User identity.\n- Group memberships.\n- Privileges.\n- Authentication information.\n- Session-specific security data.\n\n### Simplified logon process\n\n```text\n             USER\n              |\n              v\n       ENTERS CREDENTIALS\n              |\n              v\n       WINDOWS AUTHENTICATION\n              |\n              v\n       IDENTITY VERIFIED\n              |\n              v\n        LOGON SESSION\n              |\n              v\n       SECURITY TOKEN CREATED\n              |\n              v\n        WINDOWS DESKTOP\n```\n\nThe security token is then used during access-control checks.\n\n### Part 1 Summary\n\nYou have learned:\n\n- What Windows user accounts are.\n- Why individual accounts matter.\n- Local, Microsoft and domain accounts.\n- Service and built-in accounts.\n- Standard users and administrators.\n- Security Identifiers.\n- User profiles.\n- Windows logon sessions.\n\n**Progress: Part 1 of 4**\n\n---\n\n# PART 2: WINDOWS GROUPS, PERMISSIONS AND ACCOUNT POLICIES\n\n## 2.1 What Are Windows Security Groups?\n\nA security group is a collection of user accounts, computer accounts or other security principals that can receive permissions as a group.\n\nInstead of assigning permissions individually to every employee, an administrator can place users into appropriate groups.\n\nFor example, a company may have:\n\n- Finance.\n- Human Resources.\n- Developers.\n- IT Administrators.\n- Customer Support.\n\nEach group receives permissions appropriate to its responsibilities.\n\n### Example\n\nImagine a company stores documents in:\n\n```text\nC:\\Company\\\n```\n\nIt has these departments:\n\n```text\nC:\\Company\\\n       |\n       +---- Finance\n       |\n       +---- HR\n       |\n       +---- Development\n       |\n       +---- CustomerSupport\n```\n\nThe administrator can configure each folder so that only the appropriate department has access.\n\n### Why are groups important?\n\nGroups make access management:\n\n- Easier.\n- More consistent.\n- Easier to audit.\n- More scalable.\n- Less likely to contain inconsistent permissions.\n\nWhen an employee changes departments, the administrator can update their group memberships instead of manually changing many file permissions.\n\n## 2.2 Types of Windows Groups\n\nWindows groups can be understood through several categories.\n\n### A. Local Groups\n\nLocal groups are managed on a specific Windows computer.\n\nTheir membership can include local users and, where supported, domain identities and groups.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Administrators.\n- Users.\n- Guests.\n- Remote Desktop Users.\n\nLocal groups are especially useful for controlling access to a standalone computer.\n\n### B. Domain Groups\n\nDomain groups are managed through Active Directory Domain Services.\n\nThey allow administrators to manage access across organizational systems.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nFIREBOX\\Finance\nFIREBOX\\Developers\nFIREBOX\\IT-Administrators\n```\n\nThese are illustrative group names.\n\n### C. Security Groups and Distribution Groups\n\nActive Directory distinguishes between security groups and distribution groups.\n\n**Security groups** can be assigned permissions to resources.\n\n**Distribution groups** are primarily used for email distribution and are not normally used to assign resource permissions.\n\n### D. Group Scopes in Active Directory\n\nActive Directory supports group scopes.\n\n| Group Scope | General Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| Domain Local | Assign permissions to resources within its domain. |\n| Global | Organize users and groups from its own domain. |\n| Universal | Organize users and groups across multiple domains in a forest. |\n\nGroup scope determines where membership and permissions can be used.\n\n## 2.3 Important Built-in Windows Groups\n\nWindows provides built-in groups for common administrative and access-control purposes.\n\n| Group | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| Administrators | Members can perform administrative operations when appropriately elevated. |\n| Users | Ordinary user access. |\n| Guests | Restricted guest access. |\n| Remote Desktop Users | Allows members to sign in through Remote Desktop when other requirements are satisfied. |\n| Backup Operators | Provides backup and restore-related privileges. |\n| Event Log Readers | Allows reading supported event logs. |\n| Network Configuration Operators | Provides certain network configuration capabilities. |\n| Performance Monitor Users | Allows access to performance-monitoring functions. |\n| Hyper-V Administrators | Provides administrative access to Hyper-V management. |\n| Remote Management Users | Supports certain remote management operations. |\n\nThe exact group availability and behaviour depend on Windows edition and configuration.\n\n### Why should you understand built-in groups?\n\nAn attacker who gains access to a highly privileged group may gain significant control over the computer.\n\nFor example, unauthorized membership in the Administrators group can enable serious system changes.\n\nAdministrators should periodically review privileged group membership.\n\n## 2.4 Understanding Group Membership\n\nImagine a company with these employees:\n\n| Employee | Department | Group |\n|---|---|---|\n| Brian | Development | Developers |\n| Mary | Finance | Finance |\n| John | IT | IT Support |\n| Grace | HR | Human Resources |\n\nInstead of giving every employee access to every company folder, access is granted according to group membership.\n\n### Group-based access control\n\n```text\n             USERS\n               |\n       +-------+-------+\n       |       |       |\n       v       v       v\n    Finance  Developers  HR\n       |       |         |\n       v       v         v\n    Finance   Source    HR\n    Folder    Code      Folder\n```\n\nThis is an example of role-based access control.\n\nUsers receive permissions through the groups associated with their roles.\n\n## 2.5 Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)\n\nRole-Based Access Control is a method of assigning access based on organizational responsibilities.\n\nFor example:\n\n| Role | Permissions |\n|---|---|\n| Accountant | Access financial documents. |\n| Developer | Access development files. |\n| HR Officer | Access employee records. |\n| IT Administrator | Manage system configurations. |\n\nThe objective is to ensure users receive only the access required for their responsibilities.\n\n### RBAC workflow\n\n```text\n          USER\n            |\n            v\n       ASSIGNED ROLE\n            |\n            v\n       ROLE'S GROUPS\n            |\n            v\n      PERMISSIONS\n            |\n            v\n      RESOURCE ACCESS\n```\n\nRBAC is useful in both Windows environments and applications such as business management systems.\n\nFor example, BConnect could use roles such as:\n\n- Administrator.\n- Seller.\n- Landlord.\n- Tenant.\n- Customer.\n\nEach role should receive only the permissions required for its activities.\n\n## 2.6 Account Permissions and Windows Access Control\n\nWindows uses Access Control Lists to control access to protected resources.\n\nA file or folder can have permissions assigned to individual accounts or groups.\n\nImportant Windows file permissions include:\n\n| Permission | Description |\n|---|---|\n| Read | View file contents or list directory information. |\n| Write | Modify file contents or create directory entries, depending on object type. |\n| Read & Execute | Read information and execute supported files or traverse directories. |\n| Modify | Read, write and delete the object, subject to applicable controls. |\n| Full Control | Includes broad access and permission-management capabilities. |\n\n### Example: Company financial folder\n\nSuppose the company stores financial documents in:\n\n```text\nC:\\Company\\Finance\\\n```\n\nThe administrator configures:\n\n| Group | Permission |\n|---|---|\n| Finance | Modify |\n| Managers | Read & Execute |\n| Administrators | Full Control |\n| General Users | No access |\n\nThis helps prevent unauthorized employees from accessing confidential information.\n\n### Understanding inheritance\n\nWindows supports permission inheritance.\n\nA folder may pass its permissions to child folders and files.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nC:\\Company\\\n      |\n      v\nC:\\Company\\Finance\\\n      |\n      +---- report1.xlsx\n      |\n      +---- report2.xlsx\n      |\n      +---- budgets\\\n```\n\nPermissions inherited from the parent folder may apply to its contents.\n\nAdministrators should understand inheritance before changing permissions on important directories.\n\n## 2.7 Account Management Policies\n\nAccount management policies define how user accounts behave.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- Password policies.\n- Account lockout policies.\n- User rights assignments.\n- Account restrictions.\n- Security auditing policies.\n\nThese policies help organizations enforce consistent security standards.\n\n### Password policy\n\nA password policy can define:\n\n- Minimum password length.\n- Password history.\n- Password complexity requirements.\n- Maximum password age where configured.\n- Other password-related restrictions.\n\nModern password security should emphasize sufficiently long, unique passwords and multi-factor authentication where possible.\n\nFrequent forced password changes without a security reason can encourage predictable password patterns.\n\n### Account lockout policy\n\nAn account lockout policy can restrict authentication attempts after a configured number of failed attempts.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nFailed attempts: 5\nLockout duration: 15 minutes\n```\n\nThis is an illustrative policy, not a universal recommendation.\n\nLockout policies should be designed carefully because attackers may deliberately cause account lockouts to disrupt legitimate users.\n\n## 2.8 Local Security Policy\n\nWindows provides Local Security Policy on supported editions.\n\nIt allows administrators to configure local security settings.\n\nOpen it by pressing:\n\n`Windows + R`\n\nEnter:\n\n```text\nsecpol.msc\n```\n\nPress Enter.\n\nImportant sections include:\n\n| Section | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| Account Policies | Password and lockout settings. |\n| Local Policies | Audit policies, user rights and security options. |\n| User Rights Assignment | Controls which accounts can perform specific system tasks. |\n| Security Options | Configures additional operating system security behaviour. |\n| Advanced Audit Policy Configuration | Controls detailed security auditing. |\n\nSome Windows editions do not include the Local Security Policy management console.\n\n## 2.9 User Rights Assignment\n\nUser rights determine whether an account can perform specific operating system tasks.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- Log on locally.\n- Access this computer from the network.\n- Shut down the system.\n- Back up files and directories.\n- Restore files and directories.\n- Manage auditing and security logs.\n\nThese are different from ordinary file permissions.\n\nFor example:\n\nA user may have permission to read a document but still not have the right to log on to a particular server interactively.\n\n### User rights vs. file permissions\n\n| User Rights | File Permissions |\n|---|---|\n| Control specific operating system activities. | Control access to files and folders. |\n| Configured through security policy. | Configured through file-system access control. |\n| Example: Log on locally. | Example: Read a document. |\n\n## 2.10 Windows Account Lockout and Failed Logons\n\nFailed logons can happen for legitimate reasons.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Incorrect passwords.\n- Expired credentials.\n- Old passwords saved in applications.\n- Misconfigured services.\n- Unauthorized login attempts.\n\nWindows can record failed authentication activity through security auditing.\n\nAn important event is:\n\n**Event ID 4625 — An account failed to log on.**\n\nAdministrators may investigate:\n\n- Account name.\n- Logon type.\n- Source network address, when recorded.\n- Failure reason.\n- Time of occurrence.\n\nRepeated failed attempts against multiple accounts may indicate password spraying or another suspicious activity.\n\nHowever, event details must be examined before concluding that an attack occurred.\n\n### Part 2 Summary\n\nYou have learned:\n\n- Windows security groups.\n- Local and domain groups.\n- Security and distribution groups.\n- Active Directory group scopes.\n- Built-in Windows groups.\n- Group membership.\n- Role-Based Access Control.\n- File permissions and inheritance.\n- Password policies.\n- Account lockout policies.\n- Local Security Policy.\n- User Rights Assignment.\n- Failed logon investigations.\n\n**Progress: Part 2 of 4**\n\n---\n\n# PART 3: PRACTICAL WINDOWS ACCOUNT AND GROUP MANAGEMENT\n\nIn this section, you will manage accounts and groups on your own Windows computer.\n\nThe exercises use a laboratory directory and test accounts.\n\n**Important:** Do not modify real organizational accounts or privileged groups without authorization. The account-creation and group-management commands below should be performed on your own authorized Windows lab machine.\n\n## 3.1 Practical 1: List Existing Local User Accounts\n\nOpen PowerShell.\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nGet-LocalUser\n```\n\nThis displays local user accounts.\n\nYou may see:\n\n```text\nName          Enabled Description\n----          ------- -----------\nAdministrator False   Built-in account for administering the computer\nDefaultAccount False  A system-managed account\nGuest         False   Built-in guest account\nbrian         True    Local user account\n```\n\nThe actual results depend on your Windows installation.\n\n### Explanation\n\n`Get-LocalUser` retrieves local user accounts.\n\nThe output commonly includes:\n\n- Name.\n- Enabled.\n- Description.\n- SID.\n- LastLogon.\n- Password-related information.\n\n### Why is this command useful?\n\nAdministrators can use it to identify:\n\n- Enabled accounts.\n- Disabled accounts.\n- Unexpected accounts.\n- Accounts that may need review.\n\nAn unfamiliar account is not automatically malicious. Investigate its purpose and ownership.\n\n## 3.2 Practical 2: Inspect a Specific User Account\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nGet-LocalUser -Name \"brian\"\n```\n\nReplace `brian` with an existing local username.\n\nTo display selected properties:\n\n```powershell\nGet-LocalUser -Name \"brian\" |\nSelect-Object Name, Enabled, LastLogon, SID, Description\n```\n\n### Explanation\n\n- `Get-LocalUser` retrieves a local account.\n- `-Name` identifies the account.\n- `Select-Object` displays selected properties.\n\nThis helps administrators investigate individual accounts without printing every account on the computer.\n\n## 3.3 Practical 3: Create a New Local User\n\nCreate a laboratory account called `labstudent`.\n\nFirst, create a secure password prompt:\n\n```powershell\n$password = Read-Host \"Enter a password for labstudent\" -AsSecureString\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `$password` is a PowerShell variable.\n- `Read-Host` requests input.\n- `-AsSecureString` requests secure-string input rather than displaying the password as ordinary text.\n\nNow create the account:\n\n```powershell\nNew-LocalUser -Name \"labstudent\" -Password $password -FullName \"Lab Student\" -Description \"Windows security training account\"\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n| Parameter | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `-Name` | Defines the account username. |\n| `-Password` | Supplies the secure password. |\n| `-FullName` | Sets the account's display name. |\n| `-Description` | Adds an explanatory description. |\n\nThe account is created as a local user.\n\nIt does not automatically become an administrator.\n\nVerify the account:\n\n```powershell\nGet-LocalUser -Name \"labstudent\"\n```\n\n### Security principle\n\nA newly created employee account should normally receive standard-user access unless administrative privileges are specifically required.\n\n## 3.4 Practical 4: Create a Security Group\n\nCreate a local group called `FireboxDevelopers`.\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nNew-LocalGroup -Name \"FireboxDevelopers\" -Description \"Development team laboratory group\"\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `New-LocalGroup` creates a local Windows group.\n- `-Name` specifies the group name.\n- `-Description` explains the group's purpose.\n\nVerify it:\n\n```powershell\nGet-LocalGroup -Name \"FireboxDevelopers\"\n```\n\nThis group will be used to organize the test development account.\n\n## 3.5 Practical 5: Add a User to a Group\n\nAdd `labstudent` to the new group:\n\n```powershell\nAdd-LocalGroupMember -Group \"FireboxDevelopers\" -Member \"labstudent\"\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `Add-LocalGroupMember` adds an account or group to a local group.\n- `-Group` identifies the destination group.\n- `-Member` identifies the account being added.\n\nVerify membership:\n\n```powershell\nGet-LocalGroupMember -Group \"FireboxDevelopers\"\n```\n\nExpected result should include:\n\n```text\nObjectClass Name\n----------- ----\nUser        DESKTOP-01\\labstudent\n```\n\nYour computer name will differ.\n\n### Why use groups?\n\nImagine the company hires ten new developers.\n\nInstead of assigning permissions individually to all ten users, the administrator can add them to the Developers group and assign the necessary permissions to that group.\n\n## 3.6 Practical 6: Remove a User from a Group\n\nTo remove the test account:\n\n```powershell\nRemove-LocalGroupMember -Group \"FireboxDevelopers\" -Member \"labstudent\"\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `Remove-LocalGroupMember` removes a member from a local group.\n- `-Group` specifies the group.\n- `-Member` identifies the account.\n\nVerify:\n\n```powershell\nGet-LocalGroupMember -Group \"FireboxDevelopers\"\n```\n\nThe removed user should no longer appear in the membership list.\n\nThis command removes group membership, not the user account itself.\n\n## 3.7 Practical 7: Inspect Built-in Groups\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nGet-LocalGroup\n```\n\nThis displays local groups available on the computer.\n\nTo inspect Administrators:\n\n```powershell\nGet-LocalGroupMember -Group \"Administrators\"\n```\n\nTo inspect Remote Desktop Users:\n\n```powershell\nGet-LocalGroupMember -Group \"Remote Desktop Users\"\n```\n\nSome groups may not exist or may have different availability depending on Windows edition and configuration.\n\n### Why inspect the Administrators group?\n\nAn unexpected account in the local Administrators group may represent a security risk.\n\nAn attacker who obtains administrative group membership may be able to:\n\n- Install software.\n- Modify system settings.\n- Change security policies.\n- Access protected files.\n- Create additional privileged accounts.\n\nHowever, investigate membership before removing anyone. Legitimate administrators and management services may require appropriate access.\n\n## 3.8 Practical 8: Disable a User Account\n\nSometimes an employee leaves an organization or an account must be temporarily suspended.\n\nYou can disable a local account without immediately deleting it.\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nDisable-LocalUser -Name \"labstudent\"\n```\n\nVerify:\n\n```powershell\nGet-LocalUser -Name \"labstudent\"\n```\n\nThe `Enabled` property should show:\n\n```text\nFalse\n```\n\n### Why disable rather than delete?\n\nDisabling an account can preserve:\n\n- Account identity.\n- Security references.\n- Existing file ownership.\n- Audit history.\n- Information needed for an investigation.\n\nThe account can be re-enabled if necessary.\n\nHowever, disabling an account alone does not automatically revoke every previously issued credential, token or remote session. Organizations must also manage sessions, passwords, keys and connected services.\n\n## 3.9 Practical 9: Re-enable a User Account\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nEnable-LocalUser -Name \"labstudent\"\n```\n\nVerify:\n\n```powershell\nGet-LocalUser -Name \"labstudent\"\n```\n\nThe account should now show:\n\n```text\nEnabled : True\n```\n\nThis command restores the account's enabled state.\n\nIt does not automatically change the password.\n\n## 3.10 Practical 10: Change a User Password\n\nCreate a secure password prompt:\n\n```powershell\n$newPassword = Read-Host \"Enter the new password\" -AsSecureString\n```\n\nChange the password:\n\n```powershell\nSet-LocalUser -Name \"labstudent\" -Password $newPassword\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `Set-LocalUser` modifies a local account.\n- `-Name` identifies the account.\n- `-Password` supplies the new secure password.\n\nThis is useful when resetting an account password.\n\nThe operation should be performed by an authorized administrator or account-management process.\n\n## 3.11 Practical 11: Remove a Test User Account\n\nOnly perform this step after completing the exercises that require the test account.\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nRemove-LocalUser -Name \"labstudent\"\n```\n\nThis permanently removes the local account.\n\n**Important:** Removing an account is not the same as securely deleting all files previously owned by that account.\n\nFiles and other resources may remain on the computer.\n\nBefore deleting a real account, establish whether its data must be retained, transferred or archived.\n\n## 3.12 Practical 12: Manage Accounts Using Command Prompt\n\nWindows also provides the `net user` command.\n\n### List users\n\n```cmd\nnet user\n```\n\nThis lists local accounts and, in certain contexts, domain accounts depending on how the command is used.\n\n### Inspect a user\n\n```cmd\nnet user labstudent\n```\n\nThis displays account information.\n\n### Create a user\n\n```cmd\nnet user labstudent * /add\n```\n\nThe asterisk requests a password interactively rather than placing the password directly in the command.\n\n### Disable an account\n\n```cmd\nnet user labstudent /active:no\n```\n\n### Enable an account\n\n```cmd\nnet user labstudent /active:yes\n```\n\n### Delete an account\n\n```cmd\nnet user labstudent /delete\n```\n\nThese commands generally require appropriate administrative permissions when modifying accounts.\n\nPowerShell offers more structured objects and is usually more convenient for advanced account-management automation.\n\n## 3.13 Practical 13: Manage Local Groups Using Command Prompt\n\n### List local groups\n\n```cmd\nnet localgroup\n```\n\n### Inspect group membership\n\n```cmd\nnet localgroup Administrators\n```\n\n### Create a group\n\n```cmd\nnet localgroup FireboxDevelopers /add\n```\n\n### Add a user to a group\n\n```cmd\nnet localgroup FireboxDevelopers labstudent /add\n```\n\n### Remove a user from a group\n\n```cmd\nnet localgroup FireboxDevelopers labstudent /delete\n```\n\n### Delete a group\n\n```cmd\nnet localgroup FireboxDevelopers /delete\n```\n\nThe examples assume the relevant group and account exist.\n\n## 3.14 Practical 14: Configure Folder Permissions for a Group\n\nNow you will connect group management to file-system access control.\n\nCreate a laboratory folder:\n\n```powershell\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory -Path \"C:\\WindowsSecurityLab\\Development\" -Force\n```\n\nThe folder represents a company development directory.\n\nInspect its permissions:\n\n```powershell\nGet-Acl \"C:\\WindowsSecurityLab\\Development\"\n```\n\n### Grant the Developers group Modify access\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nicacls \"C:\\WindowsSecurityLab\\Development\" /grant \"FireboxDevelopers:(OI)(CI)M\"\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n| Component | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `icacls` | Manages Windows file and folder access-control lists. |\n| Folder path | Identifies the folder to modify. |\n| `/grant` | Adds an allowed permission entry. |\n| `FireboxDevelopers` | Identifies the security group. |\n| `(OI)` | Object inherit: applies to files created inside the folder. |\n| `(CI)` | Container inherit: applies to child directories. |\n| `M` | Modify permission. |\n\nThis allows the group to modify the folder's contents according to the resulting access-control rules.\n\nVerify the permissions:\n\n```powershell\nicacls \"C:\\WindowsSecurityLab\\Development\"\n```\n\n### Important note\n\nDo not blindly replace existing permissions on production folders.\n\nPermission changes should be tested because inherited entries, explicit entries and existing deny rules can affect effective access.\n\n## 3.15 Practical 15: Inspect Account Lockout and Password Policy\n\nOn supported Windows editions, open:\n\n```text\nsecpol.msc\n```\n\nNavigate to:\n\n```text\nAccount Policies\n      |\n      +---- Password Policy\n      |\n      +---- Account Lockout Policy\n```\n\nReview settings such as:\n\n- Minimum password length.\n- Password history.\n- Account lockout threshold.\n- Account lockout duration.\n- Reset account lockout counter after.\n\nYou can also inspect applicable account policies through Command Prompt:\n\n```cmd\nnet accounts\n```\n\nThis displays password and account policy information for the local computer or domain context.\n\nPolicies can be affected by domain Group Policy or other centralized management.\n\n## 3.16 Practical 16: Inspect User Account Logon Events\n\nOpen Event Viewer:\n\n```text\neventvwr.msc\n```\n\nNavigate to:\n\n```text\nWindows Logs\n     |\n     +---- Security\n```\n\nLook for these important events:\n\n| Event ID | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| 4624 | Successful logon. |\n| 4625 | Failed logon. |\n| 4634 | Account logoff. |\n| 4647 | User-initiated logoff. |\n| 4648 | Logon attempted using explicit credentials. |\n| 4672 | Special privileges assigned to a new logon. |\n| 4720 | User account created. |\n| 4722 | User account enabled. |\n| 4725 | User account disabled. |\n| 4726 | User account deleted. |\n| 4732 | Member added to a security-enabled local group. |\n| 4733 | Member removed from a security-enabled local group. |\n\nSome events depend on audit configuration and the type of account-management activity.\n\n### Retrieve recent account-creation events\n\nRun PowerShell as an administrator:\n\n```powershell\nGet-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{\n    LogName = 'Security'\n    Id = 4720\n} -MaxEvents 10\n```\n\nThis retrieves up to ten recent user-account creation events from the Security log.\n\n### Retrieve recent administrator group changes\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nGet-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{\n    LogName = 'Security'\n    Id = 4732\n} -MaxEvents 10\n```\n\nReview the results carefully.\n\nEvent 4732 is not limited to Administrators group membership. It can record members being added to different security-enabled local groups.\n\nCheck the event details to identify the actual group and member.\n\n## 3.17 Practical 17: Build a Basic User Account Inventory\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nGet-LocalUser |\nSelect-Object Name, Enabled, LastLogon, SID |\nFormat-Table -AutoSize\n```\n\nThis creates a readable account inventory.\n\nYou can save the results to a CSV file:\n\n```powershell\nGet-LocalUser |\nSelect-Object Name, Enabled, LastLogon, SID |\nExport-Csv \"$env:USERPROFILE\\Desktop\\LocalUsers.csv\" -NoTypeInformation\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `Export-Csv` exports PowerShell objects into CSV format.\n- The quoted path identifies the output file.\n- `-NoTypeInformation` removes the extra PowerShell type-information line.\n\nThe file can be opened in Excel or another spreadsheet application.\n\nAccount inventories should be handled securely because they reveal information about the computer's identities and security structure.\n\n## 3.18 Common Windows Account-Management Mistakes\n\n| Mistake | Why It Is Dangerous | Better Practice |\n|---|---|---|\n| Giving every user administrator access | Increases the impact of compromise. | Use standard accounts. |\n| Sharing one account | Reduces accountability. | Give every user an individual account. |\n| Leaving unused accounts enabled | Creates unnecessary access paths. | Review and disable unnecessary accounts. |\n| Using weak passwords | Makes guessing and credential attacks easier. | Use long, unique passwords. |\n| Ignoring group membership | May leave unauthorized privileges. | Audit privileged groups. |\n| Deleting accounts immediately | May destroy useful ownership and investigation context. | Review and preserve required data first. |\n| Running services as administrators unnecessarily | Increases the impact of service compromise. | Use dedicated least-privilege identities. |\n| Ignoring failed logons | May delay detection of suspicious activity. | Review authentication events. |\n| Forgetting remote sessions | Disabling an account may not terminate every active session. | Revoke sessions and connected credentials where necessary. |\n\n### Part 3 Summary\n\nYou have practised:\n\n- Listing local user accounts.\n- Inspecting individual accounts.\n- Creating test users.\n- Creating security groups.\n- Adding and removing group members.\n- Inspecting built-in groups.\n- Disabling and enabling accounts.\n- Resetting passwords.\n- Removing test accounts.\n- Managing accounts through Command Prompt.\n- Managing folder permissions.\n- Reviewing password and lockout policies.\n- Investigating account-management events.\n- Exporting an account inventory.\n\n**Progress: Part 3 of 4**\n\n---\n\n# PART 4: KAI ASSESSMENT, PRACTICAL PROJECT AND REVIEW\n\n## 4.1 Knowledge Assessment: Questions and Answers\n\n### Question 1\nWhat is a Windows user account?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nA Windows user account is an identity that allows a person or service to interact with a Windows computer. It has an associated security context used to control access to system resources.\n\n### Question 2\nWhy should employees have individual accounts?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nIndividual accounts improve accountability, privacy, auditing and access control. They also make it easier to investigate suspicious activities.\n\n### Question 3\nDifferentiate between local and domain accounts.\n\n**Answer:**\n\nA local account is managed on an individual computer. A domain account is managed centrally through an organizational directory such as Active Directory Domain Services.\n\n### Question 4\nWhat is a Microsoft account?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nIt is an online Microsoft identity that can connect Windows sign-in with Microsoft's cloud and application services.\n\n### Question 5\nWhat is a service account?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nA service account is an identity used by an application, background service or scheduled task to perform specific operations.\n\n### Question 6\nWhat is the difference between a standard user and an administrator?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nA standard user has restricted permissions intended for everyday activities. An administrator can perform privileged system-management operations when appropriately elevated.\n\n### Question 7\nWhat is a Security Identifier?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nA Security Identifier is a unique identifier Windows uses to represent a user, group or another security principal.\n\n### Question 8\nWhat is a user profile?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nA user profile stores user-specific information, including personal files, application settings and other configuration data.\n\n### Question 9\nWhat is a Windows security group?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nIt is a collection of security principals that can receive permissions collectively.\n\n### Question 10\nWhat is the purpose of the Administrators group?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nIt contains accounts and groups authorized to perform administrative operations on a local computer, subject to Windows elevation and security controls.\n\n### Question 11\nWhat is the purpose of the Remote Desktop Users group?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nIt grants group membership that can permit remote interactive sign-in through Remote Desktop when other system, network and policy requirements are satisfied.\n\n### Question 12\nDifferentiate between security groups and distribution groups.\n\n**Answer:**\n\nSecurity groups can be assigned permissions to resources. Distribution groups are primarily used for email distribution.\n\n### Question 13\nWhat is Role-Based Access Control?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nRBAC is an access-management approach in which permissions are assigned according to organizational roles and responsibilities.\n\n### Question 14\nWhat is group membership?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nGroup membership describes which users or other security principals belong to a particular security group.\n\n### Question 15\nWhat is permission inheritance?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nPermission inheritance allows child files and folders to receive applicable access-control entries from their parent directories.\n\n### Question 16\nWhat is a DACL?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nA Discretionary Access Control List defines which security principals are allowed or denied access to a protected object.\n\n### Question 17\nWhat is the purpose of an account lockout policy?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nIt restricts authentication after a configured number of unsuccessful attempts, helping reduce certain password-guessing attacks.\n\n### Question 18\nWhat is Local Security Policy?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nIt is a Windows management interface used to configure local security settings such as password policies, account lockout rules and user rights assignments.\n\n### Question 19\nWhat does `Get-LocalUser` do?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nIt retrieves local user accounts from the Windows computer.\n\n### Question 20\nWhat does `New-LocalUser` do?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nIt creates a new local Windows user account.\n\n### Question 21\nWhat does `Add-LocalGroupMember` do?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nIt adds a user or another supported security principal to a local Windows group.\n\n### Question 22\nWhat does `Disable-LocalUser` do?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nIt disables a local user account without deleting the account itself.\n\n### Question 23\nWhat does `Remove-LocalGroupMember` do?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nIt removes a user or another supported security principal from a local group.\n\n### Question 24\nWhat does Event ID 4720 represent?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nIt represents a user account being created, provided the relevant security auditing is enabled.\n\n### Question 25\nWhat does Event ID 4725 represent?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nIt represents a user account being disabled.\n\n### Question 26\nWhy should administrators regularly inspect the Administrators group?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nUnexpected membership may indicate excessive privileges or unauthorized access. Reviewing the group helps identify accounts that should not have administrative permissions.\n\n### Question 27\nWhy is disabling an account sometimes better than deleting it immediately?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nDisabling preserves the account identity and may help retain useful security references, ownership information and investigation context.\n\n### Question 28\nWhat is the difference between account management and permission management?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nAccount management controls user identities and their states. Permission management controls what those identities can access or perform.\n\n### Question 29\nWhy should service accounts follow least privilege?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nIf a service is compromised, a restricted service account limits the actions an attacker may be able to perform.\n\n### Question 30\nWhy is auditing important in Windows account management?\n\n**Answer:**\n\nAuditing records relevant activities such as account creation, group membership changes and authentication events. These records help administrators investigate suspicious behaviour.\n\n---\n\n## 4.2 Practical Project: Firebox Technologies Account Management System\n\n### Scenario\n\nFirebox Technologies has hired new employees and needs a secure Windows account-management structure.\n\nThe organization has four departments:\n\n1. Development.\n2. Finance.\n3. Human Resources.\n4. IT Support.\n\nEach employee needs an individual account.\n\nThe company wants employees to access only the resources needed for their jobs.\n\nYou have been assigned the role of a junior Windows administrator.\n\nYour task is to build a small account-management laboratory using local Windows users and groups.\n\n### Project Objectives\n\nYou must demonstrate that you can:\n\n1. Create local user accounts.\n2. Create departmental groups.\n3. Assign users to the correct groups.\n4. Inspect privileged group membership.\n5. Configure folder access.\n6. Disable and enable accounts.\n7. Inspect account policies.\n8. Review account-management events.\n9. Generate an account inventory.\n10. Produce a security report.\n\n### Step 1: Create Departmental Groups\n\nOpen PowerShell as Administrator.\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nNew-LocalGroup -Name \"FBX_Developers\"\nNew-LocalGroup -Name \"FBX_Finance\"\nNew-LocalGroup -Name \"FBX_HR\"\nNew-LocalGroup -Name \"FBX_ITSupport\"\n```\n\nThese commands create four local security groups.\n\nVerify:\n\n```powershell\nGet-LocalGroup |\nWhere-Object { $_.Name -like \"FBX_*\" }\n```\n\n### Step 2: Create Employee Accounts\n\nCreate the following test employees:\n\n| Username | Full Name | Department |\n|---|---|---|\n| dev_brian | Brian | Development |\n| finance_mary | Mary | Finance |\n| hr_grace | Grace | Human Resources |\n| it_john | John | IT Support |\n\nCreate each account using a secure password prompt.\n\nExample for the first employee:\n\n```powershell\n$password = Read-Host \"Enter password for dev_brian\" -AsSecureString\n\nNew-LocalUser -Name \"dev_brian\" `\n-Password $password `\n-FullName \"Brian\" `\n-Description \"Development department test account\"\n```\n\nRepeat the process for the remaining accounts using different strong passwords.\n\nDo not place actual passwords in your source code, screenshots or report.\n\n### Step 3: Assign Users to Groups\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nAdd-LocalGroupMember -Group \"FBX_Developers\" -Member \"dev_brian\"\n\nAdd-LocalGroupMember -Group \"FBX_Finance\" -Member \"finance_mary\"\n\nAdd-LocalGroupMember -Group \"FBX_HR\" -Member \"hr_grace\"\n\nAdd-LocalGroupMember -Group \"FBX_ITSupport\" -Member \"it_john\"\n```\n\nVerify each group:\n\n```powershell\nGet-LocalGroupMember -Group \"FBX_Developers\"\n\nGet-LocalGroupMember -Group \"FBX_Finance\"\n\nGet-LocalGroupMember -Group \"FBX_HR\"\n\nGet-LocalGroupMember -Group \"FBX_ITSupport\"\n```\n\nExplain why each user belongs to only the group associated with their assigned department.\n\n### Step 4: Create Departmental Folders\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory -Path \"C:\\FireboxCompany\\Development\" -Force\n\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory -Path \"C:\\FireboxCompany\\Finance\" -Force\n\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory -Path \"C:\\FireboxCompany\\HR\" -Force\n\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory -Path \"C:\\FireboxCompany\\ITSupport\" -Force\n```\n\nThese folders represent departmental resources.\n\n### Step 5: Configure Departmental Access\n\nGrant the relevant groups Modify permissions.\n\n```powershell\nicacls \"C:\\FireboxCompany\\Development\" /grant \"FBX_Developers:(OI)(CI)M\"\n\nicacls \"C:\\FireboxCompany\\Finance\" /grant \"FBX_Finance:(OI)(CI)M\"\n\nicacls \"C:\\FireboxCompany\\HR\" /grant \"FBX_HR:(OI)(CI)M\"\n\nicacls \"C:\\FireboxCompany\\ITSupport\" /grant \"FBX_ITSupport:(OI)(CI)M\"\n```\n\nInspect each folder:\n\n```powershell\nicacls \"C:\\FireboxCompany\\Development\"\n```\n\nRepeat for the other folders.\n\nExplain how group-based permissions help protect departmental information.\n\n**Important:** These commands add permissions; they do not remove existing inherited access. Before using this design in a production environment, review the complete effective permissions, inherited entries and parent-folder access. Test access using the actual departmental accounts.\n\n### Step 6: Inspect Privileged Accounts\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nGet-LocalGroupMember -Group \"Administrators\"\n```\n\nDetermine whether any test employee has been added to the Administrators group.\n\nOrdinary departmental users should not receive administrative membership automatically.\n\n### Step 7: Disable an Employee Account\n\nSimulate an employee leaving the organization.\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nDisable-LocalUser -Name \"hr_grace\"\n```\n\nVerify:\n\n```powershell\nGet-LocalUser -Name \"hr_grace\"\n```\n\nExplain why disabling the account is an appropriate initial response while the organization reviews data ownership and account-retention requirements.\n\n### Step 8: Re-enable the Account\n\nFor the laboratory exercise:\n\n```powershell\nEnable-LocalUser -Name \"hr_grace\"\n```\n\nVerify that the account is enabled again.\n\n### Step 9: Generate an Account Inventory\n\nRun:\n\n```powershell\nGet-LocalUser |\nSelect-Object Name, Enabled, LastLogon, SID |\nExport-Csv \"C:\\FireboxCompany\\AccountInventory.csv\" -NoTypeInformation\n```\n\nOpen the generated CSV file.\n\nReview the employee accounts and their status.\n\n### Step 10: Review Account-Management Events\n\nOpen Event Viewer and inspect the Security log.\n\nLook for events related to:\n\n- Account creation.\n- Account enabling.\n- Account disabling.\n- Group membership changes.\n- Successful logons.\n- Failed logons.\n\nRelevant events include:\n\n| Event ID | Activity |\n|---|---|\n| 4624 | Successful logon. |\n| 4625 | Failed logon. |\n| 4720 | User created. |\n| 4722 | User enabled. |\n| 4725 | User disabled. |\n| 4732 | User added to a security-enabled local group. |\n| 4733 | User removed from a security-enabled local group. |\n\n### Step 11: Prepare Your Security Report\n\nYour report should contain:\n\n| Section | Information Required |\n|---|---|\n| 1. Introduction | Purpose of the account-management project. |\n| 2. User Accounts | Created accounts and their descriptions. |\n| 3. Security Groups | Departmental groups. |\n| 4. Group Membership | Which users belong to each group. |\n| 5. Folder Permissions | Access rules assigned to departmental folders. |\n| 6. Privileged Accounts | Findings from Administrators group inspection. |\n| 7. Account Status | Enabled and disabled accounts. |\n| 8. Account Policies | Password and lockout settings. |\n| 9. Security Events | Account-management and authentication events. |\n| 10. Recommendations | Improvements to account security. |\n\nInclude screenshots of the PowerShell commands and outputs.\n\nDo not include passwords, recovery keys or other confidential information.\n\n---\n\n## 4.3 Lesson Completion Checklist\n\nBefore submitting this lesson, confirm that you can:\n\n- [ ] Explain Windows user accounts.\n- [ ] Differentiate local, Microsoft and domain accounts.\n- [ ] Explain service accounts.\n- [ ] Differentiate standard users and administrators.\n- [ ] Explain Windows SIDs.\n- [ ] Describe user profiles and logon sessions.\n- [ ] Explain local and domain security groups.\n- [ ] Identify important built-in Windows groups.\n- [ ] Explain Role-Based Access Control.\n- [ ] Understand NTFS permissions.\n- [ ] Explain permission inheritance.\n- [ ] Understand password policies.\n- [ ] Explain account lockout policies.\n- [ ] Use `Get-LocalUser`.\n- [ ] Create accounts using `New-LocalUser`.\n- [ ] Create groups using `New-LocalGroup`.\n- [ ] Add users using `Add-LocalGroupMember`.\n- [ ] Remove group members using `Remove-LocalGroupMember`.\n- [ ] Disable and enable accounts.\n- [ ] Manage accounts using Command Prompt.\n- [ ] Configure basic folder permissions using `icacls`.\n- [ ] Inspect account-management events.\n- [ ] Generate an account inventory.\n- [ ] Complete the Firebox Technologies project.\n\n## 4.4 Submission to Kai\n\nSubmit your practical project to Kai, the Firebox AI Instructor.\n\nYour submission should contain:\n\n1. The account-management report.\n2. Screenshots of account creation.\n3. Screenshots of departmental group membership.\n4. Screenshots of folder permissions.\n5. Screenshots showing account enablement and disablement.\n6. Your account inventory.\n7. Security event observations.\n8. Answers to the assessment questions.\n\nKai should evaluate your technical accuracy, understanding of Windows identity management, ability to configure permissions and application of least-privilege principles.\n\n---\n\n# LESSON COMPLETE\n\n**Course:** Windows Security\n\n**Lesson:** Windows Users, Groups and Account Management\n\n**Level:** Beginner to Intermediate\n\n**Progress:** Part 4 of 4 — Lesson Complete\n\n**Next Lesson:** Windows Authentication, Password Security and Multi-Factor Authentication";

export default windowsSecurityUsersGroupsAccountManagementLessonContent;
